# RER-ENGINE — Rust Engine

## Engine First

Rust is the source of truth for all engine state and calculations.

Never reimplement engine logic in TypeScript.

The editor is `rer_engine_editor` (egui/winit) and consumes the engine **in-process**.
There is no React engine bridge to extend: do not add TypeScript types or IPC
channels to support an editor/Rust change. See `shared-types.mdc`.

## 2D / 3D

Keep 2D and 3D logic separated inside `rer-engine`:

- `rer_engine_2d` (feature `engine2d`)
- `rer_engine_3d` (feature `engine3d`)
- `rer-engine-core` (shared code)
- `rer_engine_editor` (feature `editor`; hosts 2D/3D in-process)

Only place code in `rer-engine-core` when it is genuinely shared.

## GPU

GPU policy is defined in:

- `core/src/vulkan/gpu/`
- `SharedGpu` / `VulkanOptions`
- `EngineGpuProfile` (standalone profiles)

Mandatory backend:

- Vulkan (Ash) for the editor and both engines.

Do not use:

- wgpu
- OpenGL
- DX12
- WebGL
- `Backends::all()`
- automatic backend selection
- GPU backend fallback logic
- `RER_GPU_BACKEND`

## Viewport

The editor egui window (`rer_engine_editor`) hosts the 2D/3D engine in-process on the same `SharedGpu`, swapchain and command buffer. The game world is NOT rendered by React/Chromium.

Never introduce:

- A separate overlay window or Win32 position tracker
- WebGL
- Three.js
- Babylon.js
- React Three Fiber

for the game renderer.

Do not reintroduce:

- `with_embed_parent_window`
- `set_bounds` / overlay geometry
- X11 / Wayland overlay paths (ventanas hijas/overlays, en cualquier plataforma)

## Platform

Development and runtime target **Windows 10/11 and Linux (X11 and Wayland)**.
macOS and WSL are out of scope.

- Keep the existing Windows behavior and paths intact: original Win32 bodies stay
  under `#[cfg(target_os = "windows")]`; new platform-specific code goes behind
  `#[cfg(not(target_os = "windows"))]`.
- Do not add a new OS-abstraction layer; reuse the existing `cfg` guards and
  `process.platform` checks already present in the project.
- Vulkan (Ash) remains the only GPU backend on both platforms; never select a
  backend from the environment.
- Known Linux-only limits: the GPU-per-PID metric shows N/A, and global
  Ctrl/Shift OS queries are Windows-only (Linux relies on winit-tracked state).

## Editor UI (egui)

New sidebars, modals and forms must use `crate::editor::ui::components` (see that module's table). Do not drop in stock `egui::Button` / `Slider` / `Checkbox` / `CollapsingHeader` / `ui.heading`. Colors from `crate::editor::theme`. Details: `.cursor/rules/editor-egui.mdc`.